1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:00,804
Hello everyone.

2
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,308
Welcome to my talk.

3
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:08,388
Today we're going to be talking about how we are rebuilding CI for AI scale commit volume.

4
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:16,740
And this comes from a lot of experience from build and merge infrastructure that I have been working on for a decade plus at this point.

5
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:26,372
And kind of want to walk you through all the new bottlenecks we're seeing in software factories and also specifically for highly regulated industries and banks.

6
00:00:26,734 --> 00:00:31,524
How does that translate? How do we keep things more compliant and auditable while being able to move fast?

7
00:00:32,142 --> 00:00:33,060
So let's dive in.

8
00:00:34,254 --> 00:00:35,020
My name is Gautam.

9
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:41,828
I am a co-founder at CTO and Kitar and recently joined Sonar as a principal engineer after the acquisition.

10
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:48,720
Previous to Qitar, I was at Uber for almost a decade working on build and merge infrastructure for monorepos.

11
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:55,172
So everything from build, CICD, IDEs, you name it, to make sure telepost are productive.

12
00:00:55,502 --> 00:01:06,052
And now at Qitar, as part of Sonar, I'm trying to make sure that code that's been checked in is verified and all the code written by agents is of high quality before it goes in.

13
00:01:08,046 --> 00:01:12,996
So before I get started, I want to just paint a bit of history picture.

14
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:16,932
At Uber, we had agent scale commit volume before they were agents.

15
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:25,668
So what I mean by this is we had one giant monorepo and we were doing like thousands of changes a day and with very long-running builds.

16
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:34,560
This meant that if you just kept everything serialized and merged them one at a time so as not to break your main branch, you would take a

17
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:39,620
really long time, up to days, 20 days in this case, to make sure that everything was green.

18
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:47,812
And if we skipped everything and just let everyone merge to main, we would see red main all the time because changes would conflict with each other.

19
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:53,040
And even though the CI would be green on one of them combined, they might actually break.

20
00:01:53,166 --> 00:01:58,500
So we would see like our iOS monitor would be like red all the time, and that was not productive for our developers.

21
00:01:59,246 --> 00:02:08,964
To fix this, what we did was we made a system called Summit queue, which we have a paper on, and we tested all the likely possibilities of commits that are coming in

22
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:14,084
and making sure that we can like reduce the wait time so we can test these things in parallel.

23
00:02:14,670 --> 00:02:18,400
We could also predict what could break by testing all the combinations.

24
00:02:18,574 --> 00:02:27,524
So once we know that the PR might break, we might remote it back from the queue and let the author know that they have to go fix it before they can push it back up after repasing.

25
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:31,300
And finally, like to keep the trunk green,

26
00:02:32,014 --> 00:02:38,000
only if it passes with all the combination of commits coming in, it would be let in to go directly onto the trunk.

27
00:02:38,190 --> 00:02:44,400
So you'd want to make sure that the overhead was worth it for keeping our trunk green. And this is still running today at Uber.

28
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:52,196
I'm sure now we have like merge queues that GitHub came up with and all the other code hosts, but back then there was not a system, so we developed this.

29
00:02:53,774 --> 00:03:02,400
So the problem with this approach is currently all these queues that we have, so that's either CI or review, are a centralized bottleneck in the SDLC.

30
00:03:02,958 --> 00:03:10,276
So all these queues were built for human speed, and once you build it from human speed, what happens is they are not able to keep up once you go to agents.

31
00:03:10,830 --> 00:03:15,108
Because humans generate changes, used to generate changes like a few times an hour.

32
00:03:15,470 --> 00:03:18,468
So we could do testing and review one at a time.

33
00:03:19,054 --> 00:03:27,520
Now with agents, developers are writing like several PRs in many in a day, and they won't always wait for the queue.

34
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:33,572
They all want to like come in at the same time, which means your CI and review queues are piling up and having pressure.

35
00:03:34,318 --> 00:03:44,480
And after that, like the patterns of software factories and background agents triggering on like various workflows, suddenly you see like everything slowing down to a crawl because your bottlenecks

36
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:48,260
are still gonna be CI in review and they are over capacity in this case.

37
00:03:50,830 --> 00:03:53,600
So, what does this mean for teams today?

38
00:03:54,080 --> 00:04:03,044
So, just to put things into perspective, our own rate of commits has almost like 10x since we started guitar about three years ago.

39
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:10,308
And it's just like unbelievable with the same size team, we're still very small, we're able to like uh push so much more code.

40
00:04:10,702 --> 00:04:18,800
Uh and we even see this in the industry trends on guitar. So this graph shows you the number of PRs that we see that guitar reviews over time.

41
00:04:18,926 --> 00:04:22,308
And if you look at the code trajectory, it's pretty exponential.

42
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:29,892
Every month almost we're growing 25% month over month of just the amount of PRs that we have to review, fix CI and other things.

43
00:04:30,382 --> 00:04:39,172
So this is also, I think, like corroborated by evidence that uh industry players like GitHub have been putting out where they're seeing like staggering growth in PRs.

44
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:42,160
And this is now coming for every single team.

45
00:04:42,830 --> 00:04:51,812
if our small team is seeing this, I'm sure like the larger teams and banks are seeing it like at a much higher degree as they are going through their AI adoption journeys.

46
00:04:52,686 --> 00:04:54,560
So the problem I was talking about, right?

47
00:04:54,734 --> 00:05:04,790
So if agents are opening PRs and if you make them check everything through the CI system, individually they might pass, but once you kind of merge them back on domain,

48
00:05:05,294 --> 00:05:06,560
your trunk might still be broken.

49
00:05:06,766 --> 00:05:08,000
So how do you like solve for this?

50
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:11,716
Like how do you keep things green uh at this high rate of pace?

51
00:05:12,366 --> 00:05:15,524
Right? So this is very important, kind of like how we solve Summit queue.

52
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,840
The changes themselves are not testing against each other.

53
00:05:20,142 --> 00:05:27,076
So there needs to be some sort of change in either the CI system as well as how your architecture code and in the queues to be smarter to be able to solve this.

54
00:05:28,750 --> 00:05:37,124
And the problem, we're also seeing with everyone trying to adopt like software factories, which is having agents, write all the code from the spec,

55
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:45,280
and then just having humans be responsible for the spec and review, is that the factory line that exists today was built for human speed.

56
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:51,908
as I mentioned, which means that, as agents push more changes, every part of the factory line is going to be slowing down.

57
00:05:52,366 --> 00:06:00,160
So, for example, with build and test, you might have lots of rebuilds whenever computation breaks, or you might have integration tests,

58
00:06:00,462 --> 00:06:09,764
you might have flaky tests that are not reproducible locally, and that just slows everything down because you have to retry them, and it could take hours in many cases with a complex build.

59
00:06:10,606 --> 00:06:13,828
Add to the fact that now you have review and approval.

60
00:06:14,094 --> 00:06:17,680
So a lot of you probably already use like AI to review your code.

61
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:21,440
Hopefully, you're not doing every single review with humans.

62
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:24,196
You ideally are reviewing with AI as a first pass.

63
00:06:24,686 --> 00:06:34,692
Now the reviewers become the queue if you don't have that system in place. And even after you review, there might be some criteria you might want additionally to make sure that it's approved fully.

64
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:41,188
Because what's happening now in the industry is a lot of folks are just rubber stamping them because they cannot keep up with the high rate of PRs.

65
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:44,100
They are relying on other signals now to be able to just get through.

66
00:06:44,398 --> 00:06:45,444
So the process has to change.

67
00:06:46,734 --> 00:06:50,468
And the other problem right now we're having is like a lot of these checks are running.

68
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:54,400
There's a lot of records that we keep that are expiring.

69
00:06:54,606 --> 00:06:57,476
So like your old logs don't exist as they expire out.

70
00:06:57,902 --> 00:07:03,172
There is no stamp of like what was actually checked because there is just an approval stamp.

71
00:07:03,438 --> 00:07:08,960
So there needs to be some record of these things for auditability and compliance checks to happen.

72
00:07:10,542 --> 00:07:18,404
The other issue we're seeing is that as the code volume increases, the time taken for repair also is adding a ton of overhead.

73
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:24,160
what we see on our platform, at least 13% of PRs had a recorded CI failure.

74
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:34,560
This is just one or more CI failures, which means in all these cases, there is a separate round trip to go either back to the developer machine or for an agent to understand the logs,

75
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:40,720
root cause failure, fix it, push another commit, and then keep iterating till it's green.

76
00:07:40,942 --> 00:07:42,596
And this is just CI.

77
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:49,988
Because most of the time, if a human reviewer wants to look at a PR, they're not going to take a look unless CI is green, because that's the first line of defense.

78
00:07:50,606 --> 00:07:57,380
So that also like slows the review process because they can go in parallel, because usually we want to look at something once it's finalized.

79
00:07:58,702 --> 00:08:01,060
And every lap is now paying for CI and review.

80
00:08:01,294 --> 00:08:07,972
So if your review comments come in and you're doing another pass, same at break again, and it kind of like repeats the cycle.

81
00:08:09,648 --> 00:08:11,168
So, how do we make this better?

82
00:08:11,248 --> 00:08:16,100
Like, what do we do to make this problem more manageable as agents are pushing down more code?

83
00:08:16,608 --> 00:08:23,044
So, we have discovered this obviously back and too, but also in guitar stream right now.

84
00:08:23,438 --> 00:08:32,528
But The most obvious thing is of course like make your CI really quick and fast, which means that you may want to like remove a lot of the expensive checks, try to offload them to like post

85
00:08:33,088 --> 00:08:39,300
merge even, and try to make the CI step itself really quick because your repairs will be much more efficient.

86
00:08:39,758 --> 00:08:46,020
So you can parallelize things, you can chart, you can do like uh caching. We'll talk about some of the techniques that we have used as well.

87
00:08:46,608 --> 00:08:48,548
You also want to make sure the review is fast.

88
00:08:49,008 --> 00:08:55,888
So not just like a deep review, but you also want to have maybe multiple passes that are like giving quicker signal to your developers

89
00:08:56,110 --> 00:09:04,608
so they're able to like use them as a way to understand in what scenarios they may want to go deeper for higher risk changes, or maybe for like lower risk ones,

90
00:09:04,688 --> 00:09:06,468
even like let the AI approve and merge them.

91
00:09:07,310 --> 00:09:13,604
And for the checks that are already running in CI, if you can move them to the left, it's way cheaper to catch them.

92
00:09:13,902 --> 00:09:18,148
And this actually is very relevant to checks that happen even past CI.

93
00:09:18,368 --> 00:09:23,780
For example, you might have expensive regression tests or things that might fail in production.

94
00:09:24,368 --> 00:09:31,140
not every time it's possible to move things to the left, but as much as possible, if you can move them, it means you have less bottlenecks down the line.

95
00:09:31,534 --> 00:09:35,172
And locally, agents have done more context anyway to fix the issue that they're facing.

96
00:09:36,366 --> 00:09:40,004
And you also want to have policies. This is very important for regulated industries.

97
00:09:40,208 --> 00:09:51,588
So a story we have at Back at Uber is we lost the license to operate in London because there was a glitch in the software that allowed drivers to get on the platform without all the proper checks.

98
00:09:52,078 --> 00:10:02,436
And that was because of a feature flag change. So we had to put in a process in place so that every change affecting London that was going through the STLC would need some sort of like a

99
00:10:02,688 --> 00:10:08,644
lineage of okay, this is a change affect London? If so, what are the controls in place? And we were able to win our license back.

100
00:10:09,038 --> 00:10:16,288
Now, a lot of you might be facing these kind of like challenges where agents just make it much harder to understand what's happening in your code base.

101
00:10:16,368 --> 00:10:19,408
So, how do you ensure your policies are actually being respected?

102
00:10:19,648 --> 00:10:28,448
So you need to have a way to like enforce these rules beyond CI. Like just because the code compiles doesn't mean that it's following particular specifications.

103
00:10:28,528 --> 00:10:38,276
It could be like HIPAA, it could be like DORA. There's so many other policies that these agents are not going to be aware of unless your agents are enforcing it on the other

104
00:10:38,926 --> 00:10:42,564
end. And also you want to make sure that you record that it was enforced for auditability.

105
00:10:44,430 --> 00:10:47,748
So just a quick story about how we made our CI faster as well.

106
00:10:48,048 --> 00:10:55,172
So we were at a baseline, where I would say like still fast, but like very annoying when you're trying to like push more code.

107
00:10:55,568 --> 00:11:00,100
we were around like 10 minutes for our CI and we wanted to like bring that down significantly.

108
00:11:00,494 --> 00:11:03,088
So we started looking at how can we cache outputs.

109
00:11:03,408 --> 00:11:08,516
we used Rust for our build system, so we started caching as much as possible and reusing them.

110
00:11:08,910 --> 00:11:12,196
And we also looked at ways to reduce churn in the compiler.

111
00:11:12,526 --> 00:11:18,788
So we added some more fixes to ensure that the compilation part is more cacheable and is invalidated less often.

112
00:11:19,310 --> 00:11:30,928
And by adding more fixes on top of this by sharding and by removing these overheads, we were able to bring the time of CI almost to like less than a minute for incremental steps,

113
00:11:31,008 --> 00:11:37,540
which means once the cold CI is warmed up, incremental builds are way, way quick, quicker, and we can push a ton more code.

114
00:11:37,966 --> 00:11:39,328
The other issue is also review.

115
00:11:39,470 --> 00:11:47,364
So just because the CI is green, what's so funny was when we were measuring our P90 time of like PRs making into production,

116
00:11:47,968 --> 00:11:51,968
the vast majority of time was spent in review, packet rubber.

117
00:11:52,128 --> 00:12:00,848
And even today, to be honest, like most teams spend more time reviewing code and doing other kinds of testing before they are comfortable merging the code into

118
00:12:01,088 --> 00:12:01,328
main.

119
00:12:01,870 --> 00:12:05,764
So this is something that needs to be at this on top of CI as well.

120
00:12:06,254 --> 00:12:12,288
Because right now, what's happening is agents are, of course, like generating code at the same time.

121
00:12:12,622 --> 00:12:17,508
The AI agents are also like finding issues on your code review system.

122
00:12:17,902 --> 00:12:21,636
And these automated reviews are also findings that you would have to now go back and address.

123
00:12:22,158 --> 00:12:27,652
So either you have to pull context locally for your agent or have an agent on CI hydrate and fix.

124
00:12:28,206 --> 00:12:32,580
And humans are able to review whatever is left after the AI took its first pass.

125
00:12:32,878 --> 00:12:40,196
And this needs to be part of the equation so you're not wasting precious human cycles on more and more agent generated PRs.

126
00:12:42,094 --> 00:12:47,364
Here's an example for us. We were refactoring our code base to have faster CI.

127
00:12:47,662 --> 00:12:59,652
So we were using NextText to parallelize our tests, and we had this shared database that we were spinning up to make sure that we could set it up once and all our database tests would reuse it.

128
00:13:00,142 --> 00:13:09,728
But the problem was that there were some guards we didn't put in place, which meant that when CI gets very busy, there is like cross-contamination and tests might become

129
00:13:09,968 --> 00:13:10,368
freaky.

130
00:13:10,638 --> 00:13:16,068
Guitar actually is a code reviewer, it caught it on our PR before we merged it, so we didn't have to waste time after.

131
00:13:16,430 --> 00:13:22,852
And Qitar also fixed the issue for us because it suggested the fix, committed it, and added the engineer as a co-author.

132
00:13:23,246 --> 00:13:28,388
By doing this, we were able to like prevent expensive mistakes before the code made it all the way into main.

133
00:13:29,198 --> 00:13:34,948
The other thing that we do at Qitar, interestingly, and as we see more and more of our customers start doing,

134
00:13:35,408 --> 00:13:42,596
is we are using policies and criteria to decide when is it appropriate to actually even like approve changes.

135
00:13:43,086 --> 00:13:51,524
There's a lot of changes that are like routine, lower risk documentation, dependency bumps, of course, if they're not breaking any APIs, or security patches,

136
00:13:52,448 --> 00:13:57,140
even things like lower risk changes for internal tooling that can potentially be auto-approved.

137
00:13:57,888 --> 00:14:07,268
so guitar supports these things out of the box, and you can also add additional custom rules which are plain English that you can use to like get the approval and merge on.

138
00:14:07,630 --> 00:14:12,208
And we are seeing like teams who are like very AI native starting to adopt these more and more.

139
00:14:12,368 --> 00:14:23,100
We see, I think, on our platform the rate at which folks are adopting like the auto approval and auto merge grow really quickly across teams, even from large teams to like 100 plus 2,000

140
00:14:23,342 --> 00:14:23,812
plus developers.

141
00:14:24,654 --> 00:14:26,608
So here's an example of approval policies.

142
00:14:26,688 --> 00:14:30,608
So if it's a dependency bump and dock changes, we should be approving them.

143
00:14:30,798 --> 00:14:35,908
Any changes under sensitive stuff like payments or auth might need a human approver on top.

144
00:14:36,398 --> 00:14:43,300
And then, of course, you don't want to have the agent who made the change approve its change because that would not be zero trust.

145
00:14:44,110 --> 00:14:55,556
So in this case, as agents are generating PRs, the PRs are getting approved. When there is a new change that doesn't meet the criteria, then we would have to escalate to another approver.

146
00:14:55,854 --> 00:15:01,028
And this can be done with your own agents, of course, but also GitHub supposed is out of the box if you want to try

147
00:15:03,086 --> 00:15:11,204
And the other problem with just relying on CI today is that just because CI is green, as I mentioned, doesn't mean the code is compliant in many cases.

148
00:15:11,726 --> 00:15:17,444
So in this case, the CI is passing and there is some metadata that we have about

149
00:15:18,446 --> 00:15:27,620
it. On the rerun, a flaky test is going to fail, which means that when you force push to fix that thing, now the branch is pointing at another piece of code.

150
00:15:28,206 --> 00:15:33,568
So the problem with this is that over time, most CI data is going to be expired.

151
00:15:33,774 --> 00:15:40,768
So unless you have a record of what happened and what was fixed, you will not be able to be compliant or be able to audit what actually

152
00:15:40,768 --> 00:15:45,124
happened. Which is why I think things like agent trajectories and like your

153
00:15:46,350 --> 00:15:49,088
outcomes after each push are very, very important.

154
00:15:49,358 --> 00:15:56,612
So whatever information you can record on the PR or any other system of record is going to be a source of truth as your artifacts expire.

155
00:15:58,062 --> 00:16:03,780
So the control here is making sure that the change was approved and tested by someone who did not write it.

156
00:16:04,110 --> 00:16:06,084
If you don't have that information, it could be a problem.

157
00:16:07,342 --> 00:16:11,780
So here's an example. We have a pipeline that failed in the left and the pipeline that passed.

158
00:16:12,238 --> 00:16:21,220
But the difference here is that if you record properly why the failure occurred and someone else was able to approve it, because it was a known failure mode,

159
00:16:21,710 --> 00:16:24,132
and it was saved with the change that made it in.

160
00:16:24,528 --> 00:16:29,568
this thing actually passes your checks versus something where you don't have a record of what happened.

161
00:16:29,648 --> 00:16:39,524
but you just blindly trust the agent pushing code because it could be a test that was actually not high quality. There was no record of all the compliance checks that were like recorded.

162
00:16:39,822 --> 00:16:41,956
So you want to make sure that on the

163
00:16:43,310 --> 00:16:51,236
more sensitive industries, you want to have all of these decisions recorded as rules or compliance checks on top of your PR in addition to code review.

164
00:16:53,678 --> 00:16:57,088
So, what are some of the kinds of records that we want to keep?

165
00:16:57,294 --> 00:17:01,248
So, here's a few that we keep on Nokitar, which I think are just examples.

166
00:17:01,614 --> 00:17:02,660
It could be different for folks.

167
00:17:03,086 --> 00:17:07,168
So, for example, which coding agent made the PR?

168
00:17:07,374 --> 00:17:09,248
Where did the PR originate from?

169
00:17:09,518 --> 00:17:11,204
What was the session that originated it?

170
00:17:11,648 --> 00:17:13,568
And who actually started it?

171
00:17:13,728 --> 00:17:18,916
Could it be a trigger from an automated workflow or could it be just an engineer doing prompting?

172
00:17:19,470 --> 00:17:29,968
And for each of these like CIs that run, what is the commit where CI was trusted and the same test report, not just the status, and making sure that each finding has a

173
00:17:30,288 --> 00:17:35,684
record of how it was resolved, was it dismissed, was it followed up, was it like fixed on the PR?

174
00:17:36,206 --> 00:17:44,516
And then making sure the approval is someone apart from the bot that was prompted by the same person, and making sure that any overrides are explicitly named.

175
00:17:44,608 --> 00:17:53,988
So if there is a red build or like a controlled code coverage script, you want to make sure there's a reason for when, why that check failed and why we're okay with that in that scenario.

176
00:17:54,702 --> 00:18:01,728
And making sure that the code that merges actually has all this and it's not like a small change on top that was merged.

177
00:18:01,808 --> 00:18:03,332
So all the checks are on every single time.

178
00:18:04,654 --> 00:18:07,968
So in this case, we want to make sure that agents cannot approve their own changes.

179
00:18:08,206 --> 00:18:10,916
Engineer who prompted it is also not an author.

180
00:18:11,728 --> 00:18:14,372
And if you have these practices in place,

181
00:18:15,246 --> 00:18:21,828
now we can actually start removing some of these bottlenecks because the CI being faster will unblock your pills.

182
00:18:22,208 --> 00:18:24,356
So your flow is much nicer.

183
00:18:24,622 --> 00:18:31,588
And making sure you can like move some checks left will also make sure that your test load is going to be reduced quite a bit on the CI side.

184
00:18:32,174 --> 00:18:40,208
If you can automate review for the most part and only escalate to humans for exceptional cases and potentially even auto-approve

